---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A moderation queue rendered as a tall stack of horizontal bars, most of them struck through, in flat gold and black on warm cream"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will pick one volunteer project in week 1 — English Wikipedia,
    OpenStreetMap, a subreddit, or an open-source repository — and contribute to
    it every week until week 12. Real edits, in public, under your own name,
    alongside people who are not being marked.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will be reverted. You will take it to a talk page and wait for a stranger
    who owes you nothing. You will argue a deletion and then close one. From week
    7 the cohort writes the rules for a sandbox community of its own, hands half
    of them to a bot that has no judgement, and staffs the queue on a roster
    until the end of semester. In week 11 you audit what those rules actually
    did. In week 12 you write the handover note and give the whole thing to next
    year.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Postgraduate students who have already worked somewhere, because half this
    course is a labour question and it lands differently once you have had a job.
    No technical prerequisite: if you can read a diff and write a paragraph, the
    tooling will not be what stops you.
  </p>
  <p>
    It suits you if you have ever maintained something nobody thanked you for. It
    will not suit you if you want a course about social media strategy, growth,
    or community management as a profession. Nobody in this course is building an
    audience.
  </p>

  <h2>The shape of the semester</h2>
  <ol class="arc">
    <li>
      <strong>Newcomer</strong> <span>weeks 1–3</span>
      <p>Read the room before you touch anything, then make one small true edit.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contributor</strong> <span>weeks 4–6</span>
      <p>Get reverted, argue it properly, and find out who decides what exists.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Steward</strong> <span>weeks 7–9</span>
      <p>Write rules, enforce them against your classmates, and design the rota.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Handover</strong> <span>weeks 10–12</span>
      <p>Strikes, forks, bots, and leaving without taking the reasons with you.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>One thing before you enrol</h2>
  <p>
    This course puts students into communities of real people who did not
    volunteer to be coursework. There is a contributor undertaking, you sign it in
    week 1, and it is the shortest and most binding document in the course.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href={`${base}policies/`}>Read the undertaking</a> before you decide.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>Twelve rostered Shifts, and what leaves the room at the end of each.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="The Log" href="/log/">
      <p>A real entry from the 2026 cohort, annotated — the spine of the course.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="The Sandbox" href="/sandbox/">
      <p>Last year's ruleset, the config that enforces part of it, and why each rule exists.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four items, 100%, and why the shift work is worth less than it should be.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>Twelve weeks of the argument, two of them with slides.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Sixty-three thousand edits, eleven years of road surveys, one topic ban, and a bot.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .arc {
    margin-block: 1.5rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .arc li {
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--at-accent, currentColor);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .arc strong {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .arc span {
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-inline-start: 0.5rem;
  }

  .arc p {
    margin-block: 0.35rem 0;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }
</style>
